+# ADR-0001: R1 openVFIFE library boundaries
+
+- **Status:** Accepted for R1 implementation
+- **Decision date:** 2026-11-08
+- **Baseline:** `refactor/modern-cpp@32800f03a5d47f997b1a0c70460bad83fcf7faf7`
+- **Milestone:** R1 — openVFIFE C++ modernization & library refactor
+- **Parent issue:** #39
+- **Executor issue:** #40
+- **Architecture review:** #39 comment 692
+
+## Context
+
+The modernized branch has a C++20 safety baseline, RAII ownership, typed uniaxial
+material state, hardened native builds, frozen Fortran-oracle checks, and a WASM
+binding. Its repository layout still exposes implementation history rather than a
+recognizable library contract: public declarations live in `headers/`, all types
+are in the global namespace, subsystem dependencies are implicit, solver and file
+output responsibilities are mixed, and the WASM shim compiles the production
+sources directly.
+
+R1 will reorganize that code without changing verified solver behavior. This ADR
+defines the boundary first so that later issues can move files and tighten
+contracts without inventing architecture while editing formulas.
+
+The capability inventory in
+[`r1-capability-inventory.md`](r1-capability-inventory.md) is normative for what
+R1 currently calls supported, experimental, unimplemented, or internal. The
+migration map in [`r1-migration-map.md`](r1-migration-map.md) is normative for the
+first file/namespace move.
+
+## Decision
+
+### 1. One public namespace and one compiled library
+
+All supported public C++ declarations will live in the single namespace
+`openvfife`. Folder names are organizational boundaries, not nested C++
+namespaces. R1 does **not** introduce `openvfife::element`,
+`openvfife::solver`, or similar namespace layers.
+
+The canonical compiled target will be `openvfife`, exported to downstream CMake
+consumers as:
+
+```cmake
+openVFIFE::openvfife
+```
+
+R1 begins with one compiled library. Separate compiled subsystem libraries are
+not justified by the current dependency graph and would add packaging surface
+without improving correctness.
+
+### 2. Public include layout
+
+The first structural move will use these concept folders:
+
+```text
+include/
+  openvfife/
+    core/
+      particle.hpp
+    numerics/
+      quadrature.hpp
+    material/
+      material.hpp
+    section/
+      section.hpp
+      grid_section.hpp        # experimental surface; not in umbrella initially
+    element/
+      element.hpp
+    solver/
+      struct_system.hpp
+    openvfife.hpp             # small supported umbrella only
+src/
+  core/particle.cpp
+  numerics/quadrature.cpp
+  material/material.cpp
+  section/section.cpp
+  section/grid_section.cpp
+  element/element.cpp
+  solver/struct_system.cpp
+  io/                         # implementation/adapters introduced only when #46 needs them
+bindings/
+  wasm/vfife_shim.cpp
+```
+
+No empty public `io/` framework is created merely to satisfy a diagram. The
+legacy `Config` utility is internal and is moved under `src/io/` when its ODR
+hazard is corrected in #46. Public I/O headers are added only if #46 identifies
+a supported consumer contract.
+
+The optional umbrella header will initially include only supported public
+families:
+
+```cpp
+#include <openvfife/core/particle.hpp>
+#include <openvfife/numerics/quadrature.hpp>
+#include <openvfife/material/material.hpp>
+#include <openvfife/section/section.hpp>
+#include <openvfife/element/element.hpp>
+#include <openvfife/solver/struct_system.hpp>
+```
+
+`section/grid_section.hpp` remains directly includable for experimental users but
+is intentionally omitted from the supported umbrella until an integrated element
+family consumes it with adequate verification.
+
+### 3. Dependency direction
+
+The target dependency graph is deliberately small:
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  core[core] --> eigen[Eigen 3.4.0]
+  numerics[numerics]
+  material[material]
+  section[section geometry]
+  grid[section grid/integration] --> section
+  grid --> material
+  grid --> numerics
+  element[element] --> core
+  element --> material
+  element --> section
+  solver[solver] --> core
+  solver --> material
+  solver --> section
+  solver --> element
+  io[io adapters/internal config] --> solver
+  wasm[bindings/wasm] --> solver
+  wasm --> element
+```
+
+The corresponding rules are:
+
+| Area | May depend on | Must not depend on |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `core` | C++ standard library, exported Eigen headers where public types require them | numerics, material, section, element, solver, I/O, bindings, UI |
+| `numerics` | C++ standard library | solver, bindings, UI |
+| `material` | C++ standard library; low-level math only when required by its own contract | element, solver, bindings, UI |
+| `section/section` | C++ standard library | element, solver, bindings, UI |
+| `section/grid_section` | section geometry, material response, numerics | element, solver, bindings, UI |
+| `element` | core, material, section geometry | solver, I/O, bindings, UI |
+| `solver` | core, material, section, element | bindings, UI |
+| `io` | stable library read/query surface as needed | bindings, UI |
+| `bindings/wasm` | public/core library API | UI implementation details |
+
+The current `element.h` includes `gridsection.h` and `integrator.h` even though the
+modern branch no longer contains the old plastic-beam classes that required them.
+#42 should remove such accidental transitive includes while preserving behavior.
+
+### 4. Keep class names during the structural move
+
+R1 changes include paths and introduces `openvfife`, but it does not combine that
+source-breaking migration with gratuitous class renaming. Existing class names
+remain the initial public spelling (`Particle`, `LinearElastic`, `Beam3D`,
+`StructSystem`, and so on). Any later naming cleanup requires a separate API
+decision and migration plan.
+
+Global compatibility aliases are **not** added by default. They would make the
+old global namespace part of the new API indefinitely and hide incomplete caller
+migration.
+
+### 5. Ownership and observer lifetime contract
+
+`StructSystem` is the owning aggregate for objects transferred to it through
+`std::unique_ptr`. Elements observe their particles, material, and section; those
+observer references/pointers do not own the dependency.
+
+R1 preserves these invariants:
+
+- A system outlives its owned particles, materials, sections, and elements.
+- Elements must not outlive the objects they observe.
+- Element destruction occurs before the particle/material/section objects it
+  observes. The existing `StructSystem::clearOwnedObjects()` order is therefore
+  semantically significant.
+- Failed elements and free particles remain owned when moved into the existing
+  lifecycle containers.
+- The modern branch currently does not prove that every dependency referenced by
+  an element belongs to the same `StructSystem`; #44 owns that checked-membership
+  change and must test it separately.
+
+Returning a const container of `unique_ptr` does not make the pointed-to solver
+objects deeply immutable. R1 therefore makes no thread-safety promise. Parallel
+assembly, shared mutable materials, GPU execution, and a general concurrency
+framework are outside this milestone.
+
+### 6. Required versus optional section contract
+
+A beam/link structural formulation requires a `BaseSection`. A generic element
+base cannot assume that every formulation has a beam cross-section.
+
+The R1 contract is:
+
+- `StructElement` and the current link/beam families require a non-null section.
+- The common `BaseElement` representation must permit “no section”.
+- Surface formulations may carry their own thickness/section-like data.
+- Solid formulations do not receive a dummy beam section.
+- Access to an optional section must be checked/explicit; unconditional
+  dereference through `BaseElement::section()` is not the final common-base
+  contract.
+
+#43 implements and tests this decision. #40 records it only.
+
+This decision is informed by `feature/solid-tet-fracture` at
+`b83e9211cc7b3fa62712a1c3f9c2058cff583eea`, where `ShellTri3` and `SolidTet4`
+construct their plane/solid bases with a null section. That branch is integration
+evidence, not a production API to copy wholesale.
+
+### 7. Ordered element topology is explicit
+
+`BaseElement` currently exposes a `std::map<int, Particle*>`, which is ordered by
+particle ID. Map order is suitable for lookup/reporting but is not a geometric
+local-node ordering contract.
+
+Each formulation that depends on local node order must retain an explicit ordered
+connectivity representation. Existing `StructElement` already does this with
+`pi_`/`pj_`. A triangle/tetrahedron must similarly preserve `p0,p1,p2[,p3]`
+semantics independent of particle IDs. #43 owns the implementation.
+
+### 8. Six storage slots do not imply six active DOFs
+
+`Particle` stores six translational/rotational slots and a six-entry activity
+mask. This is storage, not a requirement that every formulation activate all six
+DOFs.
+
+Element families activate only the DOFs required by their formulation. Future
+solid elements may use translational DOFs only; shell formulations may require
+rotations. The common core does not encode a “frame node” assumption.
+
+### 9. Material parameters are shared; response history is not
+
+`BaseMaterial` on the modern branch is a **uniaxial** material-parameter and
+response contract despite its generic historical name. Mutable constitutive
+history belongs to the element/integration point, represented today by
+`UniaxialMaterialState` for link/section-fiber responses.
+
+R1 does not generalize this into a universal continuum constitutive interface.
+Plane-stress/plane-strain and 3D response contracts require formulation-specific
+follow-up work and verification. `ShellTri3`/`SolidTet4` from the feature branch
+must not be presented as validated merely because frame parity passes.
+
+### 10. Numerical output contracts remain formulation-specific
+
+`ElementForce`, `Strain`, and `Stress` describe the current frame/link result
+surface. They are not declared to be a universal continuum result schema. A
+future continuum contract must document stress/strain measure, component order,
+engineering versus tensor shear, local axes, units, and result location
+(node/end/integration point).
+
+R1 preserves the existing CSV schemas and WASM C ABI while isolating their
+implementation. A general results API is outside R1.
+
+### 11. Eigen policy
+
+R1 uses the vendored Eigen **3.4.0** tree under `headers/Eigen` as the default
+public dependency baseline. This keeps offline builds reproducible while the
+package layout changes. Because public APIs currently expose Eigen types, #47
+must export/include Eigen correctly for installed consumers rather than relying
+on repository-relative paths.
+
+System Eigen discovery is optional follow-up work, not an R1 blocker.
+
+### 12. WASM is a binding, not the library architecture
+
+`wasm/vfife_shim.cpp` is an external C ABI adapter over the C++ library. It moves
+to `bindings/wasm/` and must link the canonical library target rather than own a
+separate list of core `.cpp` files. C export names, Emscripten settings, generated
+module shape, and worker import expectations are compatibility constraints for
+#48.
+
+No browser/UI header or implementation becomes a dependency of the C++ core.
+
+### 13. Version and compatibility policy
+
+The repository documents a historical 1.x line: the README states licensing
+from version 1.0 and `WIKI.md` records upstream `v1.02` (2021). R1 deliberately
+changes both include paths and the C++ namespace. Those changes are
+**source-breaking**, even though verified numerical behavior is intended to be
+preserved.
+
+Therefore:
+
+- R1 does not claim source or binary ABI compatibility with the historical 1.x
+  global-namespace/header layout.
+- If this refactor is published as a versioned public release, it should use a
+  new major compatibility line (for example 2.x, with pre-releases as needed),
+  not imply that it is a drop-in 1.0x update.
+- Supported types are the compatibility surface for that release line.
+- Experimental types have no stability promise until promoted with evidence.
+- Unimplemented types are not documented as usable functionality.
+- No ABI stability guarantee is introduced by R1; compiler/standard-library/Eigen
+  ABI compatibility remains a downstream packaging concern.
+
+## Deferred work owned by later R1 issues
+
+This ADR intentionally does not implement the following:
+
+- canonical CMake/package target — #41
+- file moves and `openvfife` namespace — #42
+- topology and optional-section fixes — #43
+- closed system membership / checked IDs / safe removal — #44
+- material defaults and unsupported numerical capability handling — #45
+- I/O isolation and `Config` ODR correction — #46
+- relocatable install and Eigen export — #47
+- WASM target migration and fresh artifact validation — #48
+- CI enforcement — #49
+- final public/contributor documentation and executable examples — #50
+- continuum integration/follow-up numerical briefs — #51
+- integrated acceptance — #52
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Positive
+
+- Contributors get familiar include/source/CMake boundaries without changing
+  verified formulas during the structural move.
+- Public, experimental, unimplemented, and internal surfaces are explicit before
+  installation/export work begins.
+- Continuum work can be reconciled without forcing frame-specific assumptions
+  (mandatory beam sections, ID-sorted topology, six active DOFs) into the common
+  base.
+- WASM and UI remain consumers of the library rather than architectural owners of
+  it.
+
+### Costs
+
+- Existing C++ callers must update include paths and qualify types with
+  `openvfife::`.
+- Some historically visible declarations will no longer be promoted as supported
+  public API.
+- R1 must carry both architecture migration work and explicit compatibility
+  documentation because the change is source-breaking.
\ No newline at end of file
